of subscriptions, integrations, and transport"},"726":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/core-api-59","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/core-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/core-api#store-methods-and-fields","content":"Application code should prefer named actions over `apply()`. `apply()` is a low-level contract for\ntransports, middleware, and adapters."},"727":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/core-api-60","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/core-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/core-api#store-methods-and-fields","content":"Both forms publish a commit, so a transition made this way is visible to\n`onStoreCommit` and to everything built on it — history, sync, a devtool. With\npatches, `state` must be the state the store holds: omit it, or pass\n`getPureState()`. A patch pair describes a change to the current state, so\napplying it to a different base would leave the store somewhere its own commits\ndo not lead, and that is refused."},"728":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/core-api-61","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/core-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/core-api#computed-declaration","content":"Call `get()` with no arguments to read the current root state."},"729":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/core-api-62","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/core-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/core-api#lifecycle-helpers","content":"`onStoreReady(store, callback)` defers work until state and integrations are fully materialized.\nThe callback may return cleanup that runs during `destroy()`."},"730":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/core-api-63","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/core-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/core-api#lifecycle-helpers","content":"`wrapStore(store)` is available for framework and integration authors that need the callable\nstore shape."},"731":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/core-api-64","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/core-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/core-api#signal-primitives","content":"`coaction` and `coaction/shared` re-export advanced `alien-signals` primitives,\nincluding `signal`, `computed`, `effect`, `effectScope`, batching helpers, and type guards.\nApplication state normally uses getters or `get(deps, selector)` instead."},"732":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/core-api-65","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/core-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/core-api#adapter-entry","content":"`coaction/adapter` exposes `defineExternalStoreAdapter()` (and compatibility alias\n`createBinder()`), mutable-adapter snapshot helpers, root replacement helpers, schema errors, and\npatch path safety utilities. This surface is for integration authors, not ordinary state updates."},"733":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/core-api-66","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/core-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/core-api#adapter-entry","content":"It also exports `onStoreCommit`, `onStoreCommitValidate`, `onStoreCommitPrepare` and\n`replayStorePatches`. See Store commits for hook timing,\nvalidation, replay and lifetime."},"734":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/core-api-67","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/core-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/core-api#adapter-entry","content":"For the generated symbol-by-symbol TypeDoc output, see the\nrepository API directory."},"735":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","type":"page","content":"FAQ","breadcrumbs":["Coaction","Reference"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference/faq"},"736":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-0","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/faq","content":"Direct answers about local use, selectors, serialization, schemas, workers, adapters, and DevTools."},"737":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-1","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#can-i-use-coaction-without-workers","content":"Can I use Coaction without workers?"},"738":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-2","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#do-i-have-to-use-observer-in-react","content":"Do I have to use `observer()` in React?"},"739":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-3","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#are-getters-recomputed-on-every-read","content":"Are getters recomputed on every read?"},"740":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-4","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#why-did-a-direct-mutation-throw","content":"Why did a direct mutation throw?"},"741":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-5","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#can-i-add-state-keys-later","content":"Can I add state keys later?"},"742":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-6","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#why-does-my-client-action-return-a-promise","content":"Why does my client action return a promise?"},"743":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-7","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#why-was-my-shared-value-rejected-even-though-jsonstringify-works","content":"Why was my shared value rejected even though JSON.stringify works?"},"744":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-8","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#can-local-state-contain-cycles-and-shared-references","content":"Can local state contain cycles and shared references?"},"745":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-9","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#can-i-persist-or-undo-on-a-shared-client","content":"Can I persist or undo on a shared client?"},"746":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-10","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#can-an-adapter-be-one-slice","content":"Can an adapter be one slice?"},"747":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-11","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#does-coaction-include-signal-primitives","content":"Does Coaction include signal primitives?"},"748":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-12","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#does-coaction-have-redux-devtools-integration","content":"Does Coaction have Redux DevTools integration?"},"749":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-13","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#when-should-i-choose-zustand-instead","content":"When should I choose Zustand instead?"},"750":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-14","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#can-i-use-coaction-without-workers","content":"Yes. Local mode is the recommended starting point and exposes the full native state, action,\ncomputed, slices, and framework experience. Vanilla local-only applications should import\n`coaction`."},"751":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-15","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#do-i-have-to-use-observer-in-react","content":"No. Without `observer()`, `useStore()` is a whole-store subscription. You can also call\n`useStore(selector)` or use the cached map from `useStore.auto()`."},"752":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-16","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#do-i-have-to-use-observer-in-react","content":"`observer()` is the path that adds automatic store/slice-field tracking."},"753":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-17","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#are-getters-recomputed-on-every-read","content":"No. Native accessor getters are cached through the signal runtime and invalidated when their\ndependencies change. Keep them pure."},"754":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-18","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#why-did-a-direct-mutation-throw","content":"Coaction's public state is immutable. Write inside `set()` so the runtime can commit state,\ninvalidate computed values, notify subscribers, and produce patches when required."},"755":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-19","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#can-i-add-state-keys-later","content":"Not at the public root. Coaction fixes the root schema after initialization. Declare a record or\narray field and add dynamic entries inside it."},"756":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-20","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#why-does-my-client-action-return-a-promise","content":"A shared client does not execute the method locally. It sends the method path and arguments to the\nauthority, waits for the result, and then waits for its mirrored state to reach the authority's\nreported sequence."},"757":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-21","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#why-was-my-shared-value-rejected-even-though-jsonstringify-works","content":"`JSON.stringify()` silently normalizes or drops several values. Coaction requires a lossless JSON\ntree and rejects `undefined`, non-finite numbers, `-0`, accessors, platform objects, sparse arrays,\ncycles, and repeated references."},"758":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-22","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#can-local-state-contain-cycles-and-shared-references","content":"Yes, initialization and complete value replacement preserve them. Native recipes still use\nMutative drafts: creating cycles from draft references or editing inside cyclic drafts is\nunsupported. Build a complete graph from ordinary objects and replace its field:"},"759":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-23","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#can-local-state-contain-cycles-and-shared-references","content":"A recipe may also assign that complete value. Use `store.apply(nextState)` when replacing a\ncyclic root, and do not mutate values after passing them to the store. Acyclic shared nodes use\nindependent draft paths: changing `draft.left.value` need not change `draft.right.value`.\nAssign `draft.right = draft.left` explicitly when the result should share that node."},"760":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-24","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#can-local-state-contain-cycles-and-shared-references","content":"Commit replay and history preserve the recorded result, using snapshots when necessary. They\ndo not extend the draft editing contract, and shared transport still requires a JSON tree."},"761":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-25","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#can-i-persist-or-undo-on-a-shared-client","content":"No. Persist and history are authority-side integrations. A client-local write would create a\nsecond authority and diverge from other mirrors."},"762":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-26","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#can-an-adapter-be-one-slice","content":"No. Zustand, MobX, Pinia, Jotai, Redux, Valtio, and XState integrations are whole-store adapters.\nUse native Coaction slices or an external adapter, not both in one store."},"763":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-27","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#does-coaction-include-signal-primitives","content":"Yes, advanced `alien-signals` primitives are re-exported. Normal application derived state should\nstill use accessor getters or `get(deps, selector)`."},"764":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-28","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#does-coaction-have-redux-devtools-integration","content":"Not as a maintained built-in contract today. Use logger and project-specific tooling, and do not\npromise a DevTools surface based only on internal hooks."},"765":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/faq-29","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/faq#when-should-i-choose-zustand-instead","content":"Choose Zustand when a very small hook store, explicit selectors, minimal bundle size, and ecosystem\nmaturity are the highest priorities. Choose Coaction when automatic render tracking, maintained\ncached derivation, natural `this` actions, or a future shared runtime meaningfully reduce\napplication complexity."},"766":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","type":"page","content":"Store commits","breadcrumbs":["Coaction","Reference"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits"},"767":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-0","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","content":"Observe transitions, validate writes and replay patch pairs through Coaction's integration contract."},"768":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-1","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#commit-shape","content":"Commit shape"},"769":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-2","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#hook-timing-and-lifetime","content":"Hook timing and lifetime"},"770":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-3","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#replay-through-the-store","content":"Replay through the store"},"771":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-4","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","content":"Commit hooks are for history, persistence, synchronization and other integrations. Application\ncode normally writes through named actions and reads through framework subscriptions.\nImport these hooks from `coaction/adapter`, including when the store was created from a\ndefault local entry."},"772":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-5","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#commit-shape","content":"`StoreCommit<T>` contains the committed state and the patch pair describing the transition:"},"773":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-6","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#commit-shape","content":"Field"},"774":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-7","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#commit-shape","content":"Meaning"},"775":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-8","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#commit-shape","content":"`state`"},"776":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-9","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#commit-shape","content":"State produced by the transition; treat it as read-only"},"777":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-10","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#commit-shape","content":"`patches`"},"778":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-11","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#commit-shape","content":"Forward transition from the preceding state"},"779":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-12","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#commit-shape","content":"`inversePatches`"},"780":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-13","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#commit-shape","content":"Reverse transition back to the preceding state"},"781":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-14","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#commit-shape","content":"`source`"},"782":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-15","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#commit-shape","content":"`setState`, `mutableAction`, `external` or `replay`"},"783":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-16","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#commit-shape","content":"Both forms of `store.apply` publish commits. A listener also sees supported transitions that\nnever go through `apply`. Patches may replace a changed top-level field, or use a full root\nsnapshot at `path: []` for local graphs. A commit pair is not necessarily JSON-serializable;\nshared transport and remote sync impose their own stricter value contracts."},"784":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-17","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#hook-timing-and-lifetime","content":"API"},"785":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-18","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#hook-timing-and-lifetime","content":"When it runs"},"786":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-19","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#hook-timing-and-lifetime","content":"Purpose"},"787":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-20","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#hook-timing-and-lifetime","content":"`onStoreCommit(store, listener)`"},"788":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-21","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#hook-timing-and-lifetime","content":"After a transition commits"},"789":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-22","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#hook-timing-and-lifetime","content":"Observe accepted transitions"},"790":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-23","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#hook-timing-and-lifetime","content":"`onStoreCommitValidate(store, validator)`"},"791":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-24","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#hook-timing-and-lifetime","content":"Before a transition commits"},"792":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-25","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#hook-timing-and-lifetime","content":"Throw to refuse a write"},"793":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-26","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#hook-timing-and-lifetime","content":"`onStoreCommitPrepare(store, listener)`"},"794":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-27","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#hook-timing-and-lifetime","content":"While preparing an object-valued transition"},"795":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-28","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#hook-timing-and-lifetime","content":"Return `true` to request exact state replacement for graph handling"},"796":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-29","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#hook-timing-and-lifetime","content":"Any of these registrations requests patch generation while active, even without\n`enablePatches: true`. Each returns an idempotent unsubscribe function. Destroying the store\nreleases registrations; registering after destruction throws."},"797":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-30","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#hook-timing-and-lifetime","content":"Commit listeners run before ordinary subscriptions and effects. A reentrant write completes\ndelivery of the preceding commit before changing state again. Observer errors reach the caller\nafter commit and subscription delivery; they do not roll back an accepted transition."},"798":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-31","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#hook-timing-and-lifetime","content":"Validators must not write to the store. Throwing rejects a Coaction-mediated transition before\nit changes state. A direct mutation of an external mutable instance has already happened by\nthe time Coaction observes it, so validation cannot veto that mutation. Prepare listeners are\nan integration hook for object graph handling, not a general validation hook."},"799":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-32","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#replay-through-the-store","content":"Replay uses the current state and the full patch pair. It runs through validation, patch\nmiddleware, adapter writers and notifications; it is not a bypass for client-mirror write\nrestrictions. History should replay pairs in the correct order and avoid recording its own\nundo as a fresh user edit. The optional `setState` option supplies a middleware-scoped write\nentry when an integration needs its wrapper to observe the replay."},"800":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits-33","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/store-commits#replay-through-the-store","content":"See 4.0 migration for `apply` ownership and graph boundaries,\nor the generated API reference\nfor signatures."},"801":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/support-matrix","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/support-matrix","type":"page","content":"Support matrix","breadcrumbs":["Coaction","Reference"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference/support-matrix"},"802":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/support-matrix-0","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/support-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference/support-matrix","content":"The maintained combinations for native stores, external adapters, middleware, and Yjs."},"803":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/support-matrix-1","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/support-matrix","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference/support-matrix#native-stores","content":"Native stores"},"804":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/support-matrix-2","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/support-matrix","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference/support-matrix#external-adapters","content":"External adapters"},"805":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/support-matrix-3","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/support-matrix","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference/support-matrix#middleware-and-collaboration","content":"Middleware and collaboration"},"806":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/support-matrix-4","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/support-matrix","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference/support-matrix#framework-selector-boundaries","content":"Framework selector boundaries"},"807":{"id":"/en/docs/reference/support-matrix-5","page_id":"/en/docs/reference/support-matrix","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference/support-matrix#shared-deployment-boundary","content":"Shared deploymen